{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSoEMooakC5KcfjpUmhx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I updated the two `metadata-forensics` files and touched nothing else.\n\n1. **`reference/techniques/metadata-forensics.md`** (233 → 244 lines, a bit over the ~200 target): added a Day 27 entry for \"Packet Loss Prevention\". The artifact was a 119 B classic pcap with 3 records, and the answer is the number of complete records. The existing prebuilt `pcap.py` answered it about 22 s after start with no wrong submits. Time Trial (32 fields) and Mastery (352 answers in 11 sets) were both correct first try. I folded the Day 26 entry into the older summary paragraph. I also updated the lane overview, recognition cues, conventions and pitfalls. The variant-axes section now records how the pcaps differed between variants: three byte-order/timestamp formats (3 of 11 Mastery sets were big-endian), link types 1/101/105/147, snapshot lengths from 256 to 262144, and some truncated packets that still count. The cross-file hash-ordering question grew to six filenames.\n2. **`reference/coverage/metadata-forensics.md`**: marked row 78 `seen D27`, added a D27 summary and a revised forecast for the last days, and added rows 175–179 for the new network topics. The recognition-cues section is 59 lines. In the readiness gaps I dropped `pcap-header`, added a D27 update to the two lane-wide note gaps, and moved the already-built Windows forensics items from priority 1 to 2.\n3. **New readiness gaps**: I added 11 new gaps. Including the two already pending, 13 `prebuild` gaps are now unbuilt; the JSON parses and every gap has the same keys. Priority 1 is the network follow-on that the day-27 prompt hints at: pcapng and other capture variants in the timed kits, spotting malformed captures, decoding packets (IPs, flows, DNS/HTTP/TLS), exported logs (Zeek, Suricata, NetFlow, firewall), network evidence on the host, and matching host